      "text": "## The Big Picture"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#002",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:networking:00-overview, git-history:networking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[networking, osi model, tcp/ip, encapsulation, layering]",
        "current_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:networking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "networking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[networking]",
        "section": "networking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Networking - The Layered Model End-to-End",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "A network exists to move a chunk of application data from one process on one\nmachine to another process on a different machine, possibly on the other side of\nthe planet, across equipment owned by dozens of organizations that have never\nmet. The only way humans have ever made that tractable is **layering**: each\nlayer solves exactly one problem and hands a clean abstraction to the layer\nabove. The genius of the design is that each layer talks *logically* to its peer\non the far machine, while *physically* the data travels straight down the stack,\nacross the wire, and back up."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#003",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:networking:00-overview, git-history:networking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[networking, osi model, tcp/ip, encapsulation, layering]",
        "current_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:networking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "networking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[networking]",
        "section": "networking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Networking - The Layered Model End-to-End",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n   HOST A                          THE PATH                       HOST B\n+-----------+                                                  +-----------+\n| APP DATA  |  <===== logical peer conversation (HTTP) =====>  | APP DATA  |  L7  Application\n+-----------+                                                  +-----------+\n| TLS/QUIC  |  <===== logical peer (encryption/session) ====>  | TLS/QUIC  |  L5-6 Session/Presentation\n+-----------+                                                  +-----------+\n| TCP / UDP |  <===== logical peer (ports, reliability) ====>  | TCP / UDP |  L4  Transport\n+-----------+                                                  +-----------+\n|    IP     |  <===== logical peer (global addressing) =====>  |    IP     |  L3  Network\n+-----------+        |              |              |           +-----------+\n| ETHERNET  |        |              |              |           | ETHERNET  |  L2  Link\n+-----------+        v              v              v           +-----------+\n|   PHY     |   +--------+     +--------+     +--------+        |   PHY     |  L1  Physical\n+-----+-----+   | SWITCH |     | ROUTER |     | ROUTER |        +-----+-----+\n      |         | (L2)   |     | (L3)   |     | (L3)   |              |\n      +========>+========+====>+========+====>+========+=============>+\n       copper/    forwards      forwards by    forwards by     fiber/\n       fiber      by MAC        IP prefix      IP prefix       copper"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#004",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:networking:00-overview, git-history:networking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[networking, osi model, tcp/ip, encapsulation, layering]",
        "current_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:networking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "networking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[networking]",
        "section": "networking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Networking - The Layered Model End-to-End",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "DATA GOES DOWN A's STACK -> ACROSS DEVICES -> UP B's STACK\n   Each box on the path only reads UP TO the layer it operates on.\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#005",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:networking:00-overview, git-history:networking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[networking, osi model, tcp/ip, encapsulation, layering]",
        "current_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:networking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "networking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[networking]",
        "section": "networking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Networking - The Layered Model End-to-End",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Read this top-down for intent, bottom-up for mechanism.** The application\n*thinks* it is talking HTTP directly to the peer. In reality every layer wraps\nthe data from above in its own header, ships the bytes down, and the layer pops\nits header off on the way up. A switch only looks at L2. A router only looks at\nL3. Neither understands your TLS session — and that separation is the whole\npoint."
    },
