      "text": "## Two Models: OSI vs. TCP/IP"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#008",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:networking:00-overview, git-history:networking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[networking, osi model, tcp/ip, encapsulation, layering]",
        "current_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:networking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "networking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[networking]",
        "section": "networking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Networking - The Layered Model End-to-End",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Theory uses the 7-layer **OSI model** (ISO/IEC 7498-1). Practice uses the\n4-layer **TCP/IP model** (the Internet architecture, described in RFC 1122).\nThey map onto each other loosely; nobody implements OSI literally, but its\nvocabulary (\"a layer-3 device\", \"an L7 load balancer\") is universal."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#009",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:networking:00-overview, git-history:networking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[networking, osi model, tcp/ip, encapsulation, layering]",
        "current_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:networking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "networking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[networking]",
        "section": "networking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Networking - The Layered Model End-to-End",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n   OSI (7 layers)              TCP/IP (4 layers)        Example\n   ===============             =================        =======\n 7 Application      \\\n 6 Presentation      >----->   Application              HTTP, DNS, TLS payload\n 5 Session          /\n 4 Transport        ------->   Transport                TCP, UDP, QUIC\n 3 Network          ------->   Internet                 IP, ICMP, routing\n 2 Data Link        \\\n                     >----->   Link                     Ethernet, Wi-Fi, ARP\n 1 Physical         /\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#010",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:networking:00-overview, git-history:networking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[networking, osi model, tcp/ip, encapsulation, layering]",
        "current_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:networking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "networking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[networking]",
        "section": "networking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Networking - The Layered Model End-to-End",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "| | OSI | TCP/IP |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Origin** | ISO standards body, ~1984 | DARPA / Internet, codified RFC 1122 (1989) |\n| **Layers** | 7 | 4 |\n| **Where TLS lives** | \"Layer 5-6\" (informally) | Inside Application, above Transport |\n| **Used for** | teaching, vendor marketing (\"L7 firewall\") | the actual Internet |\n| **Honest take** | a reference vocabulary | the implemented architecture |"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#011",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:networking:00-overview, git-history:networking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[networking, osi model, tcp/ip, encapsulation, layering]",
        "current_path": "networking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:networking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "networking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[networking]",
        "section": "networking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Networking - The Layered Model End-to-End",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "> **Bridge — abstraction layers you already know.** This is the same move as a\n> language runtime stack: your C# `Stream` doesn't know whether it's backed by a\n> file, a socket, or memory; each layer below exposes a uniform contract. OSI\n> layering is the network's equivalent of interface segregation — every layer is\n> an interface, and devices implement only the interfaces they need."
    },
